==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fro|theologien}}, equivalent to {{af|en|theology|-an}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/θi.əˈloʊd͡ʒən/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-theologian.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who studies [[theology]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1999|author=Diana S. Richmond Garland|title=Family ministry: a comprehensive guide|page=45
|passage=During a burping contest to celebrate wonderfully cold soft drinks after a long hike one hot afternoon, one little girl said, "My dad can outburp any of you!" Her father is a distinguished '''theologian''' and church leader whom I will not name here.}}
# In [[Roman Catholic]] usage, a theological [[lecturer]] attached to a [[cathedral]] [[church]].
#: {{synonyms|en|theologus}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1861|author=John Tulloch|title=English Puritanism and its leaders|page=29
|passage=Of all the '''theologians''' of his age, Hooker was the most unpuritan; {{...}}}}
# In [[Eastern Orthodox]] usage, a person who has had direct experience of and unity with [[God]].

